    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d627">

                <pb facs="0601" n="589" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg589"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">751.61/313: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-01-11T12:00:00+00:00">January 11,
                            1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received January 11—9:18 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>58. There has been a considerable demand from the Eight in France recently for
                    the breaking of diplomatic relations with the Soviet Union.</p>
                <pb facs="0602" n="590" xml:id="pg590"/>
                <p>Coulondre<anchor n="66" xml:id="d627-f1"/> stated to me yesterday that
                        Daladier<anchor n="67" xml:id="d627-f2"/> had now decided not to break
                    relations with the Soviet Union but to use the present wave of emotion against
                    the activities of the Soviet Union to crush the Communist agents of the Soviet
                    Union in France.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d627-f1" n="66" xml:id="d627-f1-content">
                <p>Robert Coulondre, Director of the Cabinet of the Minister for Foreign Affairs;
                    formerly French Ambassador in Germany, 1938–39.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d627-f2" n="67" xml:id="d627-f2-content">
                <p>Edouard Daladier, President of the French Council of Ministers until his
                    resignation on March 20, 1940.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
